1
00:00:03,603 --> 00:00:05,105
what I have to say is a pretty good

2
00:00:05,105 --> 00:00:09,509
representation of what the foreign policy
elite in Russia is thinking.

3
00:00:09,909 --> 00:00:14,247
The West is leading
Ukraine down the primrose

4
00:00:14,247 --> 00:00:18,385
path, and the Israelis
are executing a genocide in Gaza.

5
00:00:18,451 --> 00:00:22,122
It's not China's fault
that it wants to be powerful.

6
00:00:22,155 --> 00:00:25,125
So if you get into trouble and you

7
00:00:25,125 --> 00:00:28,461
dial 911, there's nobody at the other end.

8
00:00:29,262 --> 00:00:30,997
Hi this is John Mearsheimer.

9
00:00:30,997 --> 00:00:36,369
I would like to speak today
in this episode about my recent trip

10
00:00:36,403 --> 00:00:42,208
to Russia, which took place
between September 8th and September 12th.

11
00:00:42,242 --> 00:00:46,212
Those were the days that
I was in the country not traveling there.

12
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:52,052
I started off in Moscow
and I spent a few days in Moscow,

13
00:00:52,052 --> 00:00:56,222
and then the second half of my trip
was in Saint Petersburg.

14
00:00:56,956 --> 00:00:59,759
And what exactly did I do there?

15
00:00:59,759 --> 00:01:02,729
I gave a series of talks.

16
00:01:02,729 --> 00:01:06,666
I participated in some roundtable
discussions.

17
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:11,037
I interviewed a number of people

18
00:01:11,037 --> 00:01:14,040
who I have known over the years.

19
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:17,877
I had dinner one
evening with the Foreign Minister,

20
00:01:17,877 --> 00:01:23,416
Sergey Lavrov, which lasted for
well over two hours at his residence.

21
00:01:23,983 --> 00:01:29,255
And I spent a lot of time
wandering around during the daytime

22
00:01:29,255 --> 00:01:34,961
and in the evening in both Moscow and
Saint Petersburg, because I like to walk.

23
00:01:35,161 --> 00:01:39,666
And I wanted to see both of those cities,
or at least as much of those cities

24
00:01:39,666 --> 00:01:42,969
as I could see by walking around.

25
00:01:43,536 --> 00:01:46,539
Who exactly did I talk to?

26
00:01:46,573 --> 00:01:50,810
I talked to a large number of people

27
00:01:51,144 --> 00:01:54,214
who I would describe as being members

28
00:01:54,247 --> 00:01:58,184
of the foreign policy elite inside of Russia.

29
00:01:58,384 --> 00:02:03,223
I talked to a number of people who I know,
going back to the days of the Cold War,

30
00:02:03,690 --> 00:02:07,227
when they were Soviet citizens,
not Russian citizens.

31
00:02:07,494 --> 00:02:11,931
As I said, I talked to Foreign Minister
Lavrov and I talked to his deputy,

32
00:02:11,931 --> 00:02:15,869
who I have talked to in the past
when I went to Russia.

33
00:02:16,236 --> 00:02:20,140
And I think I got a pretty good feel
from talking to everyone

34
00:02:20,507 --> 00:02:25,411
that I just mentioned of sort
of how the Russian elite in general

35
00:02:25,445 --> 00:02:31,251
is thinking about the present situation
regarding the Ukraine war.

36
00:02:31,584 --> 00:02:34,587
But nevertheless, having said
all that, you should understand

37
00:02:34,587 --> 00:02:37,557
that what I have to say
is largely anecdotal.

38
00:02:37,557 --> 00:02:40,560
I didn't go in and do a survey

39
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:43,763
of what elite
opinion is on a particular issue,

40
00:02:43,796 --> 00:02:49,068
and I certainly didn't talk to 
large numbers of people in the street.

41
00:02:49,302 --> 00:02:53,640
So this is anecdotal,
but I think what I have to say

42
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:57,544
is a pretty good representation
of what the foreign policy elite in

43
00:02:57,544 --> 00:02:59,078
Russia is thinking.

44
00:02:59,078 --> 00:03:03,616
Let me start by talking about what I saw
regarding daily life

45
00:03:03,683 --> 00:03:07,720
inside of Moscow and Saint Petersburg.

46
00:03:08,288 --> 00:03:11,291
I would say it was remarkably normal

47
00:03:11,524 --> 00:03:15,461
that there were few signs of a war
taking place.

48
00:03:15,995 --> 00:03:19,532
Walking around in those two cities
is not much different

49
00:03:19,532 --> 00:03:25,171
than walking around in places
like New York or places like Chicago.

50
00:03:26,406 --> 00:03:29,108
There were a few signs of the war.

51
00:03:29,108 --> 00:03:34,547
However, I think the most obvious sign
was that at a number of what

52
00:03:34,547 --> 00:03:40,086
they call petrol stations or what
we call gas stations, you saw long lines.

53
00:03:40,086 --> 00:03:43,223
In some cases, the lines

54
00:03:43,223 --> 00:03:46,226
included maybe 15 or 20 cars.

55
00:03:46,492 --> 00:03:51,864
In one case, I think the line
probably included about 40 to 50 cars.

56
00:03:51,931 --> 00:03:53,199
That was not true

57
00:03:53,199 --> 00:03:57,403
at every petrol station or gas station,
but it was true at a number of them.

58
00:03:57,804 --> 00:04:01,808
And this is consistent
with what we read in the newspapers,

59
00:04:02,208 --> 00:04:07,380
some other signs of the war
that I would note.

60
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:10,650
First of all, the sanctions have had

61
00:04:10,650 --> 00:04:15,321
a significant effect in the sense
that if you go into Russia,

62
00:04:15,722 --> 00:04:19,492
you cannot use your own credit card

63
00:04:20,026 --> 00:04:24,797
or an American credit card,
and you can't use American money.

64
00:04:24,864 --> 00:04:28,868
I did not go in to Russia realizing that.

65
00:04:29,168 --> 00:04:33,806
So I was in fact, effectively penniless
when I was in Russia.

66
00:04:33,806 --> 00:04:38,611
If I went to a store or went to a coffee
shop, I had to make sure

67
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:43,016
I went with somebody who could pay for me
because I had no ability to pay.

68
00:04:43,783 --> 00:04:45,485
I couldn't use any of my credit cards.

69
00:04:45,485 --> 00:04:47,587
I couldn't use American dollars.

70
00:04:47,587 --> 00:04:51,357
You could only use Russian credit cards
or rubles.

71
00:04:51,624 --> 00:04:54,627
And I didn't have any of them.

72
00:04:54,894 --> 00:04:57,897
Correspondingly,

73
00:04:58,097 --> 00:05:02,402
the Russians cannot
use American credit cards.

74
00:05:02,769 --> 00:05:06,039
So if you're a Russian
and you want to subscribe

75
00:05:06,039 --> 00:05:10,043
to the New York Times,
you can't use an American credit card

76
00:05:10,143 --> 00:05:14,447
to subscribe because American credit cards
can't be used in Russia.

77
00:05:14,614 --> 00:05:17,417
So you would have to use
your Russian credit card.

78
00:05:17,417 --> 00:05:21,054
But the problem is you can't use
a Russian credit card in the West.

79
00:05:21,454 --> 00:05:26,959
So any Russian who wants to subscribe to
the New York Times or the Wall Street

80
00:05:26,959 --> 00:05:30,596
Journal or some Substack site

81
00:05:30,596 --> 00:05:34,267
cannot do it through normal channels.

82
00:05:34,567 --> 00:05:37,570
So you see the effect of sanctions there.

83
00:05:38,104 --> 00:05:41,240
One other
sign of the effect of sanctions was

84
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:45,611
that there were a number of coffee shops
that you could tell that were once

85
00:05:45,611 --> 00:05:50,583
Starbucks coffee shops
that are now star coffee shops,

86
00:05:50,817 --> 00:05:55,221
but have an emblem that resembles
or a symbol that resembles

87
00:05:55,488 --> 00:05:59,125
the old Starbucks
symbol that was once there.

88
00:06:01,027 --> 00:06:03,696
But nevertheless, despite

89
00:06:03,696 --> 00:06:06,766
the long lines that I saw
at a few gas stations,

90
00:06:06,766 --> 00:06:10,236
and despite the problem with credit cards
and using dollars,

91
00:06:10,603 --> 00:06:16,843
there were hardly any signs
that normal life

92
00:06:16,843 --> 00:06:21,180
was not going on inside of both Moscow
and Saint Petersburg.

93
00:06:21,381 --> 00:06:24,517
If you went into restaurants,
you looked around

94
00:06:24,784 --> 00:06:29,255
in department stores or drugstores,
and he walked around on the streets.

95
00:06:29,322 --> 00:06:32,024
Everything looked remarkably normal.

96
00:06:32,024 --> 00:06:34,494
Just one final point on this.

97
00:06:34,494 --> 00:06:38,197
Before the war started on February 22nd.

98
00:06:38,731 --> 00:06:41,734
Excuse me. February 24th of 2022.

99
00:06:41,801 --> 00:06:46,506
It was possible to fly from the
United States or from Europe into Russia.

100
00:06:46,606 --> 00:06:48,307
That's no longer the case.

101
00:06:48,307 --> 00:06:52,011
So I had to fly from Chicago to Istanbul

102
00:06:52,044 --> 00:06:55,047
and then from Istanbul into Russia.

103
00:06:55,081 --> 00:06:58,084
Because you cannot fly directly
from any place in the west,

104
00:06:58,117 --> 00:07:01,120
inside to inside Russia.

105
00:07:01,621 --> 00:07:04,190
Let me talk a little bit
about the economy.

106
00:07:04,190 --> 00:07:08,861
As I'm sure almost everybody knows,
when the war started.

107
00:07:09,228 --> 00:07:13,399
Our goal
was to bring the Russian economy.

108
00:07:13,866 --> 00:07:17,837
To to bring the Russian economy

109
00:07:17,837 --> 00:07:21,541
to its knees, to do egregious damage
to the Russian economy.

110
00:07:21,607 --> 00:07:23,109
It was widely believed

111
00:07:23,109 --> 00:07:27,013
that that would help
us, in effect, win the war against Russia.

112
00:07:27,613 --> 00:07:32,785
You saw no signs
that the Russian economy is in trouble.

113
00:07:33,186 --> 00:07:37,256
This is not to argue that 
everything is hunky dory

114
00:07:37,290 --> 00:07:41,427
with the Russian economy,
but it did not seem to me

115
00:07:41,427 --> 00:07:45,465
that there was any evidence
that the Russian economy was struggling.

116
00:07:46,399 --> 00:07:48,401
And there was nobody that I

117
00:07:48,401 --> 00:07:52,405
talked to
who talked about economic problems

118
00:07:52,405 --> 00:07:56,375
or dangers ahead
regarding the Russian economy.

119
00:07:56,776 --> 00:08:00,847
There may be lurking problems there
that people are concerned about,

120
00:08:00,847 --> 00:08:02,415
and they just didn't tell me.

121
00:08:02,415 --> 00:08:05,117
But all I would say is that the whole time
I was there,

122
00:08:05,117 --> 00:08:08,855
I saw no signs
that the Russian economy is in trouble.

123
00:08:08,921 --> 00:08:12,124
In fact, it looked like
the Russian economy is doing quite well.

124
00:08:12,158 --> 00:08:17,563
And again, nobody in in the time I was
there

125
00:08:17,563 --> 00:08:20,733
said that the Russians were concerned
about where

126
00:08:20,766 --> 00:08:23,769
their economy was headed.

127
00:08:23,903 --> 00:08:29,408
Now, I would note that the situation in

128
00:08:29,408 --> 00:08:34,247
Russia is fundamentally different
than the situation in Ukraine.

129
00:08:34,447 --> 00:08:39,218
There were just many times when I was
observing what was going on in Russia.

130
00:08:39,385 --> 00:08:45,625
I thought to myself, how does this compare
to what's going on in Ukraine?

131
00:08:45,858 --> 00:08:51,597
And it's quite clear that the Ukrainians
are in a very different situation.

132
00:08:51,597 --> 00:08:55,668
And in fact,
one of the days that I was in Russia,

133
00:08:55,668 --> 00:09:00,072
a headline appeared in the New York Times
that said that the Russians desperate.

134
00:09:00,139 --> 00:09:00,573
Excuse me.

135
00:09:00,573 --> 00:09:04,844
The Ukrainians desperately needed $27

136
00:09:04,844 --> 00:09:10,016
billion
more in economic and military aid.

137
00:09:10,049 --> 00:09:13,052
They were, in effect,
running out of money.

138
00:09:13,319 --> 00:09:18,758
And what this points up
is that the Ukrainian economy

139
00:09:19,058 --> 00:09:23,729
is basically been wrecked and Ukraine is.

140
00:09:25,998 --> 00:09:28,568
In need of great

141
00:09:28,568 --> 00:09:31,771
support from the West to survive.

142
00:09:32,104 --> 00:09:36,242
The Ukrainians could not fight the war
without massive amounts

143
00:09:36,242 --> 00:09:39,779
of economic assistance from the West.

144
00:09:40,146 --> 00:09:43,182
Situation is fundamentally different
in Russia.

145
00:09:43,215 --> 00:09:46,919
Russia is not depending
on assistance from anyone.

146
00:09:46,919 --> 00:09:51,424
And as I said, I do not believe the
Russian economy is in desperate straits.

147
00:09:51,424 --> 00:09:53,125
There is no evidence of that.

148
00:09:53,125 --> 00:09:56,429
It seems to be humming along
relatively well.

149
00:09:56,429 --> 00:10:01,200
And again, that situation contrasts
with the situation in Ukraine.

150
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:04,737
And furthermore, I had no sense
that there were food shortages

151
00:10:04,770 --> 00:10:08,040
or that people were suffering in
any meaningful way

152
00:10:08,407 --> 00:10:12,278
inside of Russia, whereas
there's no question that that is the case

153
00:10:12,345 --> 00:10:16,282
inside of Ukraine, 
especially in recent months

154
00:10:16,282 --> 00:10:20,886
with the shutdown of the Black Sea
ports in Ukraine.

155
00:10:21,220 --> 00:10:24,256
So there's a fundamental difference here
between the two.

156
00:10:24,457 --> 00:10:28,894
One final point on
that has to do with weaponry.

157
00:10:29,095 --> 00:10:33,799
It's quite clear that not only is
the Russian economy in quite good shape,

158
00:10:33,799 --> 00:10:38,204
but the Russians are producing
massive amounts of weaponry.

159
00:10:38,237 --> 00:10:40,906
People talked about that quite often.

160
00:10:40,906 --> 00:10:43,909
The number of weapons that are coming off

161
00:10:43,976 --> 00:10:46,979
the assembly lines is quite impressive.

162
00:10:47,046 --> 00:10:50,583
And again,
the exact opposite is the case in Ukraine.

163
00:10:50,650 --> 00:10:53,653
Ukraine is desperate for weapons
from the West.

164
00:10:53,653 --> 00:10:56,822
You hear the Ukrainians constantly
talking about

165
00:10:56,822 --> 00:11:01,293
the need for Patriot missiles,
the so-called Pac three missiles,

166
00:11:01,293 --> 00:11:05,931
to help them deal with the Russian
onslaught of missiles and drones.

167
00:11:05,998 --> 00:11:09,502
But the West is in no position
to give Ukraine.

168
00:11:09,869 --> 00:11:13,005
The weaponry it needs to deal with

169
00:11:13,005 --> 00:11:16,008
those aerial attacks from Russia.

170
00:11:17,043 --> 00:11:20,379
But on the Russian side, again,
there's no shortage

171
00:11:20,379 --> 00:11:24,116
of weaponry, both defensive missiles
and offensive missiles.

172
00:11:24,617 --> 00:11:27,720
So, the contrast between

173
00:11:27,720 --> 00:11:31,090
Russia and Ukraine is quite stark.

174
00:11:31,290 --> 00:11:35,428
And I think the Ukrainians
are in real trouble.

175
00:11:35,428 --> 00:11:39,165
And as I say, when you talk
about the economy, I think in the

176
00:11:39,165 --> 00:11:43,069
Russian case,
things are in pretty good shape.

177
00:11:43,602 --> 00:11:46,038
Now, let's talk a little bit
about a little bit

178
00:11:46,038 --> 00:11:49,375
about the political situation
inside of Russia.

179
00:11:49,875 --> 00:11:53,612
It has long been argued in the West

180
00:11:53,679 --> 00:11:56,682
that Putin is in political trouble

181
00:11:57,183 --> 00:12:00,086
and that he may be toppled.

182
00:12:00,086 --> 00:12:04,290
And of course, the belief in the West
is that if Putin is toppled,

183
00:12:04,290 --> 00:12:08,694
he will be replaced
by a more reasonable person.

184
00:12:09,061 --> 00:12:14,800
And by that, most people in the West
believe that his replacement would be

185
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:20,973
someone who was willing to negotiate
an end to the war with the West.

186
00:12:21,006 --> 00:12:25,077
That person would be more dovish
or softer than Putin,

187
00:12:25,211 --> 00:12:29,682
who was widely seen in
the West as highly aggressive.

188
00:12:30,449 --> 00:12:33,686
Now, I pushed people very hard

189
00:12:33,686 --> 00:12:39,158
on this particular issue
and people would talk about it.

190
00:12:39,158 --> 00:12:44,063
But I do want to emphasize that I think
there are limits to what people would say.

191
00:12:44,430 --> 00:12:48,167
I think most people are reluctant

192
00:12:48,167 --> 00:12:52,238
to be too critical
of Putin for obvious reasons.

193
00:12:53,239 --> 00:12:57,576
So, I'm, I'm aware that

194
00:12:57,676 --> 00:13:00,579
people were not completely frank

195
00:13:00,579 --> 00:13:03,582
on this particular issue.

196
00:13:03,682 --> 00:13:08,320
Nevertheless, I saw no evidence of

197
00:13:09,789 --> 00:13:14,960
the belief that Putin was in trouble

198
00:13:15,027 --> 00:13:21,300
politically
and that he was likely to be toppled

199
00:13:21,767 --> 00:13:26,505
because of dissatisfaction
with his leadership.

200
00:13:27,106 --> 00:13:32,411
Now, it may be the case that behind
the scenes, forces are at play

201
00:13:32,444 --> 00:13:35,648
that are going to bring

202
00:13:35,648 --> 00:13:39,118
Putin down at some point
in time for one reason or another.

203
00:13:39,185 --> 00:13:42,454
But all I would say is I did not see

204
00:13:42,454 --> 00:13:45,724
a hint of that argument.

205
00:13:45,724 --> 00:13:51,297
Now, there's no question
that there has been dissatisfaction

206
00:13:51,297 --> 00:13:54,333
for Putin for a few years now.

207
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:58,037
You want to remember the war has been
going on for more than four years.

208
00:13:58,037 --> 00:14:00,172
We're now into the fifth year of the war.

209
00:14:00,172 --> 00:14:03,008
And I would say over the past two,
maybe three years,

210
00:14:03,008 --> 00:14:06,011
there has been dissatisfaction with Putin.

211
00:14:06,178 --> 00:14:08,881
But the dissatisfaction has to do

212
00:14:08,881 --> 00:14:13,385
with the fact that many people believe,
and they will say this to you,

213
00:14:13,485 --> 00:14:19,058
that he has not been waging the war
vigorously enough,

214
00:14:19,358 --> 00:14:22,294
that he is not been tough enough

215
00:14:22,294 --> 00:14:26,065
in attacking Ukraine,

216
00:14:26,065 --> 00:14:30,069
both on the battlefield
and with the air war.

217
00:14:30,903 --> 00:14:33,906
Now, it's important to emphasize

218
00:14:34,106 --> 00:14:39,879
that that situation
has been rectified since about June.

219
00:14:40,246 --> 00:14:45,317
The Russians have greatly ramped up
their attacks on Ukraine,

220
00:14:45,584 --> 00:14:49,722
and it is unequivocally clear
that they're going to increase

221
00:14:50,756 --> 00:14:52,124
the intensity

222
00:14:52,124 --> 00:14:55,728
of the attacks on Ukraine moving forward.

223
00:14:55,961 --> 00:15:01,133
So that dissatisfaction with Putin,
it once existed because many people felt

224
00:15:01,133 --> 00:15:04,403
he was not waging the war was sufficient,

225
00:15:04,536 --> 00:15:07,539
with sufficient vigor has gone away.

226
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:10,542
So the dissatisfaction

227
00:15:10,542 --> 00:15:13,379
is, in effect, gone away.

228
00:15:13,379 --> 00:15:17,416
Now, all of this tells you that

229
00:15:17,416 --> 00:15:21,287
if Putin were to be replaced,

230
00:15:21,487 --> 00:15:25,124
let's assume that Putin
had a heart attack and could not continue

231
00:15:25,124 --> 00:15:31,096
being the Russian president, and somebody
came into office to replace him.

232
00:15:31,563 --> 00:15:34,867
That person would be
at least as hawkish as him,

233
00:15:34,867 --> 00:15:39,538
if not more hawkish, 
even if Putin had been replaced

234
00:15:39,538 --> 00:15:43,042
a year or two ago,
the person who replaced him

235
00:15:43,042 --> 00:15:47,613
would not have been more interested
in accommodating the West.

236
00:15:48,047 --> 00:15:51,617
There's no evidence
that the widespread belief in the West

237
00:15:51,884 --> 00:15:56,722
that Putin's replacement would have been
something of a pussy cat was correct.

238
00:15:56,722 --> 00:16:01,126
If anything, Putin's replacement
would have been a hard liner.

239
00:16:01,126 --> 00:16:05,731
But the point I'm making to you
now is that Putin has become a hardliner

240
00:16:05,731 --> 00:16:09,268
when it comes to the whole question
of how to wage this war.

241
00:16:09,568 --> 00:16:13,439
And the Russians are waging the war
very vigorously.

242
00:16:14,006 --> 00:16:18,277
One other point about the politics
that's worth making.

243
00:16:18,610 --> 00:16:22,314
It's very clear
from talking to Russians and,

244
00:16:22,314 --> 00:16:26,852
listening to what they have to say

245
00:16:26,986 --> 00:16:30,923
about their country and what's going on.

246
00:16:31,256 --> 00:16:36,695
They're very nationalistic
that all sorts of people

247
00:16:36,695 --> 00:16:41,467
will talk about Russian civilization
in a proud way.

248
00:16:41,467 --> 00:16:44,636
They will distinguish Russia
from the West.

249
00:16:45,204 --> 00:16:48,440
And it's very clear
that a good number of Russians

250
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:54,580
that I talk to do not like the West,
because they do not like liberal values

251
00:16:55,781 --> 00:16:58,784
and the values that they privilege.

252
00:16:59,118 --> 00:17:01,687
Focus more on things like civilization,

253
00:17:01,687 --> 00:17:05,691
excuse me, like religion,
Christianity in particular,

254
00:17:05,958 --> 00:17:10,062
the family one would call
traditional values

255
00:17:10,095 --> 00:17:15,000
a lot of emphasis on traditional values
among people that I spoke to.

256
00:17:15,100 --> 00:17:19,004
And those people sometimes distinguish
the traditional values

257
00:17:19,004 --> 00:17:24,009
that they privilege in Russia
from liberal values in the West.

258
00:17:24,343 --> 00:17:28,847
In a funny way,
when I talk to a number of Russians

259
00:17:28,847 --> 00:17:32,184
about this subject of Russian nationalism,

260
00:17:32,284 --> 00:17:35,287
they sounded a lot like JD Vance.

261
00:17:35,888 --> 00:17:39,358
So that's what I would say
about the political situation

262
00:17:39,358 --> 00:17:42,461
inside of Russia
based on my conversations.

263
00:17:42,728 --> 00:17:45,964
Now, let me say some words on

264
00:17:46,865 --> 00:17:49,902
what I think
the course of the war looks like.

265
00:17:51,170 --> 00:17:53,839
I think, as I said, the Russians

266
00:17:53,839 --> 00:17:57,409
this past summer decided to escalate
in a massive way.

267
00:17:57,876 --> 00:18:01,780
As I've talked about in
previous episodes,

268
00:18:02,114 --> 00:18:05,117
the Russians have basically

269
00:18:05,117 --> 00:18:08,120
turned Ukraine into a landlocked country,

270
00:18:08,187 --> 00:18:11,123
and they have shut down

271
00:18:11,123 --> 00:18:14,493
all the Ukrainian Black Sea ports,

272
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:18,764
which is causing massive economic problems
inside of Ukraine.

273
00:18:18,897 --> 00:18:22,668
The Russians have also made it clear
that they're going to try

274
00:18:22,701 --> 00:18:25,637
to totally destroy the energy

275
00:18:25,637 --> 00:18:28,640
infrastructure inside of Ukraine.

276
00:18:29,108 --> 00:18:33,078
And what's important to understand here

277
00:18:33,178 --> 00:18:38,484
is that for political reasons,
the Russians have no choice

278
00:18:38,784 --> 00:18:40,953
but to escalate.

279
00:18:40,953 --> 00:18:44,857
Putin clearly understands,

280
00:18:44,923 --> 00:18:47,893
and the Russian elite clearly understands,

281
00:18:47,893 --> 00:18:52,731
that it's politically unacceptable
to let this war just go on and on,

282
00:18:52,865 --> 00:18:57,202
and that it's politically unacceptable
to let the Ukrainians

283
00:18:57,202 --> 00:19:01,206
attack targets
inside of Russia with their drones.

284
00:19:01,206 --> 00:19:03,642
And there's no question
that that is happening.

285
00:19:03,642 --> 00:19:08,847
Although I saw virtually no evidence
of those Ukrainian drone attacks

286
00:19:09,014 --> 00:19:14,520
when I was in either Saint Petersburg
or in Moscow, save for the long

287
00:19:14,520 --> 00:19:18,824
gasoline for the long lines
at the gasoline station.

288
00:19:19,024 --> 00:19:23,095
But other than that,
there was no evidence of

289
00:19:23,095 --> 00:19:27,699
the air campaign
that Ukraine is waging against Russia.

290
00:19:27,766 --> 00:19:30,969
Nevertheless, it's quite clear
the Russians consider this

291
00:19:30,969 --> 00:19:35,040
to be intolerable,
and they are bent on ending it.

292
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:37,276
They are bent on ending the war.

293
00:19:37,276 --> 00:19:40,546
And the way they're going to do
that is to escalate.

294
00:19:40,612 --> 00:19:44,316
So the politics of the conflict
leave the Russians

295
00:19:44,316 --> 00:19:47,386
no choice but to escalate and escalate.

296
00:19:47,386 --> 00:19:48,720
They will.

297
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:52,524
Now, when you talk about what
this all leads to,

298
00:19:52,724 --> 00:19:56,728
the Russians will make it clear
that they intend to win the war,

299
00:19:57,596 --> 00:20:00,332
and they're actually quite confident
about that.

300
00:20:00,332 --> 00:20:04,403
I was a bit taken aback
by how sure of themselves

301
00:20:04,403 --> 00:20:07,439
the Russian elites were that
they were going to win the war.

302
00:20:07,706 --> 00:20:11,577
And I think that's due in part, again,
based on my conversations,

303
00:20:11,777 --> 00:20:16,014
to the fact that the Russians believe
the Ukrainians are in deep trouble.

304
00:20:16,848 --> 00:20:21,386
And that Russia can ramp up
the pressure on Ukraine

305
00:20:21,486 --> 00:20:25,357
and basically bring this war to an end.

306
00:20:25,824 --> 00:20:30,729
Now, let me talk a little bit
about what escalation means here.

307
00:20:31,330 --> 00:20:36,735
I think the Russians see themselves
as putting great pressure on Ukraine,

308
00:20:36,802 --> 00:20:40,706
up on the front lines,
on what we like to call the battlefield.

309
00:20:41,173 --> 00:20:45,544
And there's a clear understanding that

310
00:20:46,378 --> 00:20:49,281
there are significant cracks

311
00:20:49,281 --> 00:20:52,584
in the front line
forces on the Ukrainian side,

312
00:20:52,651 --> 00:20:56,622
and that the Russians
are making significant progress.

313
00:20:56,855 --> 00:20:58,657
But the progress is slow.

314
00:20:58,657 --> 00:21:02,160
And everybody will tell you,
unsurprisingly,

315
00:21:02,160 --> 00:21:04,396
that that's because of the drones.

316
00:21:04,396 --> 00:21:09,401
There's no question that the drones
have changed the nature of.

317
00:21:10,068 --> 00:21:13,305
Of of what's happening

318
00:21:13,338 --> 00:21:16,341
on the battlefield in quite profound ways.

319
00:21:16,608 --> 00:21:20,612
A number of Ukrainians said to me
that if there were no drones,

320
00:21:20,612 --> 00:21:24,082
this war would have ended two
maybe three years ago.

321
00:21:24,449 --> 00:21:25,784
I think that's correct.

322
00:21:25,784 --> 00:21:31,056
I think it would have ended more like 1
or 2 years ago if there were no drones.

323
00:21:31,356 --> 00:21:34,760
Drones have allowed the Ukrainians

324
00:21:34,960 --> 00:21:38,263
to hold off the Russians
on the battlefield.

325
00:21:38,530 --> 00:21:42,668
So when the Russians talk now
about escalating and finishing

326
00:21:42,668 --> 00:21:48,707
off the Ukrainians, the emphasis
is not so much on the battlefield itself.

327
00:21:49,007 --> 00:21:52,010
The battlefield defined in rather narrow
term,

328
00:21:52,477 --> 00:21:56,281
in rather narrow terms, talking about
what's happening up on the front lines.

329
00:21:56,548 --> 00:22:01,053
I think the Russians believe
that they will deliver the coup de grace

330
00:22:01,253 --> 00:22:04,489
by basically wrecking Ukraine.

331
00:22:04,890 --> 00:22:08,894
In terms of its economic and military

332
00:22:08,894 --> 00:22:12,397
infrastructure behind the front lines,

333
00:22:12,764 --> 00:22:17,636
I think that the Russians believe that
cutting off Ukraine from its Black Sea

334
00:22:17,703 --> 00:22:22,708
ports, destroying its energy
infrastructure, destroying all sorts

335
00:22:22,708 --> 00:22:27,846
of military infrastructure in rear areas
to include things like bridges,

336
00:22:28,680 --> 00:22:33,685
railroad lines, locomotives, gas stations
and so forth

337
00:22:33,685 --> 00:22:39,257
and so on will do so much damage
to the Ukrainian economy

338
00:22:39,424 --> 00:22:45,330
and to the support system
behind the Ukraine front line forces,

339
00:22:45,464 --> 00:22:49,201
that those front line
forces will just collapse

340
00:22:49,534 --> 00:22:53,238
and Russia will win the war.

341
00:22:53,238 --> 00:22:58,443
So what I'm trying to say here is that
what's happening up on the battlefield,

342
00:22:58,443 --> 00:23:02,347
narrowly defined,
does matter for sure to the Russians.

343
00:23:02,381 --> 00:23:06,651
But I think at this point in time,
the Russians tend to believe

344
00:23:06,651 --> 00:23:11,022
that the strategic bombing
campaign is their real ace in the hole.

345
00:23:11,123 --> 00:23:14,860
And now that they have decided
since the summer of this year

346
00:23:14,960 --> 00:23:17,929
to really ramp up that campaign,

347
00:23:17,929 --> 00:23:21,700
they think they can bring the Ukrainians
to their knees.

348
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:23,301
And just one final point

349
00:23:23,301 --> 00:23:26,772
about this, to tie
it back to the discussion of the economy.

350
00:23:27,038 --> 00:23:33,145
The fact that the Russians
have this ability to produce vast amounts

351
00:23:33,145 --> 00:23:39,384
of weaponry, vast around amounts
of missiles and drones and aircraft

352
00:23:39,985 --> 00:23:43,688
and so forth
and so on, is what I think gives them

353
00:23:43,688 --> 00:23:46,691
confidence that they can wreck

354
00:23:46,958 --> 00:23:50,328
the Ukrainian economy and they can wreck

355
00:23:50,362 --> 00:23:55,100
that military support system
behind the front lines as well.

356
00:23:56,067 --> 00:23:58,770
My final
point here, and I said this before,

357
00:23:58,770 --> 00:24:03,542
but I think it's important to emphasize,
is that almost everybody I talked to

358
00:24:03,575 --> 00:24:06,611
was very confident
that Russia is going to win this war.

359
00:24:08,079 --> 00:24:09,848
Let me switch gears now and talk

360
00:24:09,848 --> 00:24:13,285
about how the Russians
think about their adversaries.

361
00:24:13,285 --> 00:24:16,855
I think with regard

362
00:24:16,855 --> 00:24:20,959
to the Ukrainians,
I did not have the sense

363
00:24:20,959 --> 00:24:24,396
that there was any deep hatred inside

364
00:24:24,463 --> 00:24:27,466
of Russia towards the Ukrainians.

365
00:24:27,899 --> 00:24:31,169
And the reason is that

366
00:24:31,436 --> 00:24:35,173
many Russians who I talked to emphasize

367
00:24:35,507 --> 00:24:39,811
that they had relatives
who lived in Ukraine.

368
00:24:40,278 --> 00:24:45,584
There are huge numbers of Russians
who have both.

369
00:24:45,684 --> 00:24:50,455
One might call a Ukrainian heritage
and a Russian heritage.

370
00:24:50,822 --> 00:24:54,826
And lots of Russians
that I talked to viewed

371
00:24:54,893 --> 00:24:58,663
Ukrainians as blood
brothers and blood sisters.

372
00:24:58,864 --> 00:25:01,867
This is not to say that
they don't understand that there's a war

373
00:25:01,867 --> 00:25:06,037
taking place between Ukraine and Russia,
and there's obviously

374
00:25:06,037 --> 00:25:10,242
a huge amount of animosity
that comes with that on the Russian side.

375
00:25:10,642 --> 00:25:15,847
But I do think that on the Russian side,
anyway, that animosity was somewhat

376
00:25:15,847 --> 00:25:21,586
qualified by the fact that Russians
and Ukrainians, ethnic Russians and ethnic

377
00:25:21,786 --> 00:25:24,756
Ukrainians have been intermingled

378
00:25:25,090 --> 00:25:27,726
or have intermingled over time.

379
00:25:27,726 --> 00:25:30,428
And therefore,
there are lots of people who have both

380
00:25:30,428 --> 00:25:33,398
Russian and Ukrainian heritage.

381
00:25:33,665 --> 00:25:38,770
And that, I think, ameliorate
the level of animosity.

382
00:25:39,204 --> 00:25:44,543
And by the way, many people, I'm sure,
remember the famous article

383
00:25:44,543 --> 00:25:50,015
that Vladimir Putin
wrote on July 12th, 2021.

384
00:25:50,448 --> 00:25:54,119
This is about six months
before the war broke out.

385
00:25:54,653 --> 00:25:58,924
Putin wrote this famous article
where he emphasized

386
00:25:59,558 --> 00:26:05,330
that Ukrainians and Russians
were part of one large family.

387
00:26:05,564 --> 00:26:09,100
And he did not see a clear distinction

388
00:26:09,167 --> 00:26:13,305
between Russians on one hand
and Ukrainians on the other hand.

389
00:26:13,838 --> 00:26:16,441
What I'm saying here
is that in my conversations

390
00:26:16,441 --> 00:26:20,145
with Russia, Russians
during my recent trip,

391
00:26:20,211 --> 00:26:24,649
there were lots of people who basically
were making the same argument

392
00:26:24,649 --> 00:26:29,788
that Putin made in that famous
July 12th, 2021.

393
00:26:30,221 --> 00:26:33,625
Article that he wrote.

394
00:26:34,025 --> 00:26:39,331
So I would just say that when it comes
to Russian views of the Ukrainian people

395
00:26:39,397 --> 00:26:44,603
and the country of Ukraine, 
the animosity is somewhat qualified.

396
00:26:44,603 --> 00:26:45,770
And I don't want to overstate

397
00:26:45,770 --> 00:26:48,907
the case here, because I understand that
a war is taking place.

398
00:26:49,574 --> 00:26:53,878
Now, putting the Ukrainians aside
and focusing on the Europeans.

399
00:26:54,212 --> 00:26:57,582
The Europeans are the ultimate bad guys
for the Russians.

400
00:26:57,782 --> 00:27:00,685
You see that very clearly with everyone
you talk to.

401
00:27:00,685 --> 00:27:06,091
And this is certainly true in the dinner
that I had with Foreign Minister Lavrov.

402
00:27:06,458 --> 00:27:11,730
The Europeans are seen as being bent
on destroying

403
00:27:11,763 --> 00:27:15,333
Russia, knocking Russia
out of the ranks of the great powers.

404
00:27:15,567 --> 00:27:18,737
And I think the Russians believe
that the Europeans

405
00:27:18,737 --> 00:27:21,973
want to use Ukraine to achieve that goal.

406
00:27:22,340 --> 00:27:26,645
The Russians believe that the Europeans
themselves don't want to do the fighting,

407
00:27:26,645 --> 00:27:29,781
but they want the Ukrainians
to do the fighting and dying,

408
00:27:30,148 --> 00:27:34,452
all for the purposes of wrecking Russia
as a great power.

409
00:27:35,086 --> 00:27:37,222
To take this a step further.

410
00:27:37,222 --> 00:27:42,127
It's very clear that the Russians view

411
00:27:42,827 --> 00:27:46,931
the British and the Germans
as the ultimate bad guys.

412
00:27:47,198 --> 00:27:50,669
They believed that Britain has long been

413
00:27:50,702 --> 00:27:53,838
the driving force behind European policy.

414
00:27:54,105 --> 00:27:57,342
And now the Germans
under Chancellor Merz

415
00:27:57,709 --> 00:28:00,712
have become a huge problem.

416
00:28:00,745 --> 00:28:02,547
You want to remember that

417
00:28:03,815 --> 00:28:05,316
the Second World

418
00:28:05,316 --> 00:28:10,121
War is emblazoned in the brains
of almost everyone

419
00:28:10,288 --> 00:28:14,259
in the Russian elite,

420
00:28:14,259 --> 00:28:17,896
and the Second World War involves

421
00:28:18,530 --> 00:28:21,533
the events on the Eastern Front

422
00:28:21,900 --> 00:28:27,505
between June 22nd, 1941 and May 8th, 1945.

423
00:28:27,639 --> 00:28:31,443
And the Russians are well aware

424
00:28:31,676 --> 00:28:36,347
of the enormous damage,
the death and destruction

425
00:28:36,581 --> 00:28:39,851
that the Germans inflicted on Russia.

426
00:28:40,085 --> 00:28:45,323
So Germany is a very special threat

427
00:28:46,024 --> 00:28:48,960
to Russia because of what's happened,

428
00:28:48,960 --> 00:28:51,963
because of what happened in World War two.

429
00:28:52,197 --> 00:28:55,533
So when Chancellor Merz begins
to talk about

430
00:28:56,067 --> 00:28:59,003
the Russians as the font of all evil,

431
00:28:59,003 --> 00:29:02,373
and he begins to emphasize

432
00:29:02,440 --> 00:29:06,244
that something has to be done
to bring the Russians to their knees.

433
00:29:06,377 --> 00:29:09,781
This resonates in all sorts of ways,

434
00:29:09,781 --> 00:29:12,784
negative ways with the Russians.

435
00:29:12,817 --> 00:29:16,054
So what I'm saying to you is,
from a Russian point of view,

436
00:29:16,054 --> 00:29:20,959
it's the Europeans
who are the real devils in this story.

437
00:29:21,326 --> 00:29:24,863
And it is the British and the Germans

438
00:29:24,996 --> 00:29:29,934
among the European countries
who the Russians loathed the most.

439
00:29:30,301 --> 00:29:32,537
Then we get to the Americans.

440
00:29:32,537 --> 00:29:36,374
I think that there's no question that

441
00:29:37,776 --> 00:29:40,178
the Russians understand

442
00:29:40,178 --> 00:29:45,784
that the Americans started out to wreck

443
00:29:46,684 --> 00:29:52,423
Russia as a great power to knock,
Russia off the list of great powers.

444
00:29:52,423 --> 00:29:54,626
That was true in the Biden administration.

445
00:29:54,626 --> 00:29:59,397
But I think that most Russians I talked
to do believe that President Trump

446
00:29:59,464 --> 00:30:03,735
was interested
in trying to work out a deal

447
00:30:04,169 --> 00:30:09,607
with the Russians to not only end the war
in Ukraine, but to improve relations

448
00:30:09,607 --> 00:30:13,244
between Russia on one hand
and the United States on the other hand.

449
00:30:13,545 --> 00:30:15,280
I think the Russians understood that.

450
00:30:15,280 --> 00:30:18,316
Trump, to put it

451
00:30:18,316 --> 00:30:22,187
in general terms, he had good intentions.

452
00:30:22,220 --> 00:30:25,156
He was not Joe Biden.

453
00:30:25,156 --> 00:30:28,626
But I think that the Russians
came to believe

454
00:30:28,860 --> 00:30:33,565
that Trump was incapable
of reaching an agreement

455
00:30:33,898 --> 00:30:37,135
that Trump and Steve Witkoff

456
00:30:37,135 --> 00:30:40,138
and Jared
Kushner are basically incompetent.

457
00:30:40,238 --> 00:30:44,375
There's no way
that they can settle this war.

458
00:30:44,843 --> 00:30:48,646
And what happened at Anchorage

459
00:30:48,680 --> 00:30:51,683
is just evidence of this.

460
00:30:51,716 --> 00:30:53,852
So let me talk a little bit
about Anchorage.

461
00:30:53,852 --> 00:30:57,789
And remember here,
what I'm trying to do is explain to you

462
00:30:57,789 --> 00:31:01,092
why the Russians have come to think

463
00:31:01,426 --> 00:31:04,896
that the Americans
are basically stumble bumps.

464
00:31:04,896 --> 00:31:09,434
And here we're talking about
the Americans under President Trump.

465
00:31:10,602 --> 00:31:15,173
The Anchorage meeting in August of 2025.

466
00:31:15,206 --> 00:31:19,143
Remember, President Trump comes
into the white House in January 2025,

467
00:31:19,177 --> 00:31:24,849
and he begins to go to work
to try and settle the war in Ukraine.

468
00:31:24,849 --> 00:31:29,387
He had promised, as I'm sure everybody
remembers, that he was going to settle it

469
00:31:29,520 --> 00:31:34,125
even before he got in the white House,
if he could.

470
00:31:34,292 --> 00:31:38,196
And once he got back in the white House,
he would settle it very quickly.

471
00:31:38,296 --> 00:31:39,964
So he went to work to do that.

472
00:31:39,964 --> 00:31:43,167
President Trump shortly
after moving back into the white House.

473
00:31:43,735 --> 00:31:46,938
And, over the course

474
00:31:46,938 --> 00:31:50,208
of the summer of 2025,

475
00:31:50,608 --> 00:31:56,447
he said, Steve Witkoff
to Moscow and Witkoff met with Putin.

476
00:31:56,648 --> 00:32:01,519
And it turns out that they basically
worked out a compromise settlement.

477
00:32:02,120 --> 00:32:06,224
And then President Trump

478
00:32:06,257 --> 00:32:10,728
and President Putin met at Anchorage

479
00:32:10,728 --> 00:32:13,731
in mid-August of 2025

480
00:32:13,965 --> 00:32:16,768
for the purposes of shaking hands.

481
00:32:16,768 --> 00:32:20,038
And agreeing

482
00:32:20,204 --> 00:32:23,207
between themselves
that the deal that Wyckoff

483
00:32:23,574 --> 00:32:28,279
and Putin had worked out
was satisfactory to both sides.

484
00:32:28,947 --> 00:32:32,650
And it's important to emphasize
that this was a compromise deal.

485
00:32:32,984 --> 00:32:36,321
The Russians actually made a number

486
00:32:36,321 --> 00:32:39,857
of compromises
that I did not think they would make.

487
00:32:40,291 --> 00:32:43,494
And at the time,
this is in mid-August 2025.

488
00:32:43,494 --> 00:32:48,766
I didn't believe the stories
that Putin had agreed

489
00:32:48,866 --> 00:32:53,538
to, the deal that had been worked out
between the two sides.

490
00:32:53,771 --> 00:32:57,442
It just didn't make sense to me
that Putin would be willing

491
00:32:57,508 --> 00:32:59,344
to make such compromises.

492
00:32:59,344 --> 00:33:02,947
But it was quite clear from talking
to the Russians, and especially

493
00:33:02,947 --> 00:33:07,652
from talking to Foreign Minister Lavrov,
that the Russians did compromise.

494
00:33:07,986 --> 00:33:12,590
And basically with happened
here, is that

495
00:33:13,291 --> 00:33:17,261
the Russians made some important
compromises regarding territory.

496
00:33:17,562 --> 00:33:19,864
And let me explain what I mean by that.

497
00:33:19,864 --> 00:33:24,936
There were four oblasts
that the Russians had annexed,

498
00:33:25,136 --> 00:33:29,707
before President Trump
moved back into office in January 2025.

499
00:33:30,241 --> 00:33:33,378
And two of those

500
00:33:33,611 --> 00:33:36,447
oblasts are in the Donbas,

501
00:33:36,447 --> 00:33:39,851
and the other two were Zaporizhzhia
and Kherson.

502
00:33:39,984 --> 00:33:45,223
And the Russians had annexed the territory
and all four of those oblasts.

503
00:33:45,690 --> 00:33:48,693
But the deal
that had been worked out in Anchorage

504
00:33:48,960 --> 00:33:51,696
was that the Ukrainians

505
00:33:51,696 --> 00:33:54,465
would withdraw from the territory

506
00:33:54,465 --> 00:33:59,337
in the Donbas,
the two oblasts in the Donbas.

507
00:33:59,604 --> 00:34:01,839
They would withdraw from that territory.

508
00:34:01,839 --> 00:34:06,244
And, of course, the Russians
would take control of the entire Donbas.

509
00:34:07,445 --> 00:34:09,947
But the Russians agreed that

510
00:34:09,947 --> 00:34:13,317
the line of conflict

511
00:34:13,818 --> 00:34:18,222
in Zaporizhzhia
and the line of conflict in Kherson

512
00:34:18,322 --> 00:34:23,261
would remain in place, and in effect,
the Russians would not demand

513
00:34:23,294 --> 00:34:27,698
that they get control
of all of Zaporizhzhia and all of Kherson,

514
00:34:27,698 --> 00:34:32,170
even though they had formerly annexed
both of those oblasts.

515
00:34:32,303 --> 00:34:35,807
So the Russians were making an important
compromise

516
00:34:35,907 --> 00:34:39,210
on territory
regarding Zaporizhzhia and Kherson.

517
00:34:39,210 --> 00:34:41,546
And this is what
I didn't think they would agree to.

518
00:34:41,546 --> 00:34:43,181
But I was wrong.

519
00:34:43,181 --> 00:34:47,018
The Russians made this deal with Trump,

520
00:34:47,485 --> 00:34:51,022
and they had every reason to believe
that Trump was committed

521
00:34:51,022 --> 00:34:55,726
to the deal, in large part
because it was proposed by Steve Wyckoff.

522
00:34:56,360 --> 00:35:00,731
Just for important to understand that
this compromise was proposed by Witkoff

523
00:35:00,765 --> 00:35:05,570
before the Anchorage
meeting agreed to by Witkoff and Trump

524
00:35:05,570 --> 00:35:11,342
before Anchorage, and then agreed to at
Anchorage by Trump and Putin.

525
00:35:12,477 --> 00:35:15,179
And what happened

526
00:35:15,179 --> 00:35:21,385
was Trump was unable,
after the meeting in Anchorage,

527
00:35:21,486 --> 00:35:27,658
to get the Ukrainians
and the Europeans to agree to the deal.

528
00:35:27,758 --> 00:35:30,761
Trump had told Putin

529
00:35:31,062 --> 00:35:34,966
that he could get the Ukrainians
and the Europeans on board.

530
00:35:34,999 --> 00:35:36,901
Putin and Trump both understood,

531
00:35:36,901 --> 00:35:40,104
of course, that the two of them
agreeing to a compromise deal

532
00:35:40,238 --> 00:35:44,242
was a giant first step in terms
of getting a peace settlement.

533
00:35:44,242 --> 00:35:48,279
But you had to get the Europeans and
you had to get the Ukrainians on board.

534
00:35:48,346 --> 00:35:50,681
Trump was confident he could do that.

535
00:35:50,681 --> 00:35:56,921
But what happened is, after Anchorage, 
the Europeans and the Ukrainians

536
00:35:56,921 --> 00:36:01,359
and the hardliners in the United States
all went to work on Trump.

537
00:36:01,459 --> 00:36:03,127
And Trump backed off.

538
00:36:03,127 --> 00:36:06,330
He walked away, in effect, from Anchorage.

539
00:36:07,098 --> 00:36:09,167
And I would argue,

540
00:36:10,168 --> 00:36:11,903
by the way, that it was

541
00:36:11,903 --> 00:36:15,806
remarkably foolish of the Ukrainians
to walk away from this deal.

542
00:36:15,840 --> 00:36:18,743
This was hardly
a perfect deal for the Ukrainians,

543
00:36:18,743 --> 00:36:22,013
because they were going to end up
surrendering quite a bit of territory.

544
00:36:22,213 --> 00:36:26,250
But I think from Ukraine's point of view,
it would have been much better

545
00:36:26,584 --> 00:36:30,488
to accept that deal
than to accept the deal

546
00:36:30,488 --> 00:36:33,991
that they're going to have to accept
when this war finally ends.

547
00:36:34,025 --> 00:36:37,962
They're going to be much worse off
when this war finally ends than

548
00:36:37,962 --> 00:36:42,366
they would have been had they accepted
the agreement worked out in Anchorage.

549
00:36:42,533 --> 00:36:45,836
But be that as it may, the fact is,

550
00:36:45,836 --> 00:36:52,210
that the the deal never materialized
because Trump could not get the Ukrainians

551
00:36:52,410 --> 00:36:56,614
and the Europeans and the hardliners
in the United States to sign on

552
00:36:56,781 --> 00:37:00,518
to the arrangement
that had been worked out in Anchorage.

553
00:37:01,018 --> 00:37:06,090
The the end result of this
is that the Russians have lost all faith

554
00:37:06,090 --> 00:37:09,493
in President Trump's ability to negotiate.

555
00:37:09,961 --> 00:37:13,264
I mean, Putin may still meet
with Wyckoff and Kushner, but

556
00:37:13,297 --> 00:37:14,999
he's just going through the motions.

557
00:37:14,999 --> 00:37:20,137
The Russians understand full
well that the Americans are not capable

558
00:37:20,137 --> 00:37:23,741
of delivering, and therefore this war

559
00:37:24,308 --> 00:37:27,278
has to be settled militarily.

560
00:37:27,511 --> 00:37:31,148
It has to be settled on the battlefield.

561
00:37:31,382 --> 00:37:34,385
And with the air war.

562
00:37:34,452 --> 00:37:36,988
This is the one two punch
that the Russians

563
00:37:36,988 --> 00:37:40,891
are relying on to win this war.

564
00:37:41,359 --> 00:37:44,161
Now,

565
00:37:44,161 --> 00:37:47,198
let me close here by talking about what

566
00:37:47,198 --> 00:37:50,568
I think the final deal will look like.

567
00:37:51,102 --> 00:37:54,105
I've emphasized for a couple of years

568
00:37:54,105 --> 00:37:57,875
now that the Russians would win
an ugly victory.

569
00:37:57,908 --> 00:38:01,779
This has been my argument
all along, and I used this rhetoric

570
00:38:01,779 --> 00:38:05,049
in my conversations
with a number of Russians.

571
00:38:06,317 --> 00:38:09,320
I asked them,

572
00:38:09,420 --> 00:38:12,957
point after point,
what the final deal would look like.

573
00:38:12,957 --> 00:38:17,228
What do they think 
the settlement is going to look like?

574
00:38:17,261 --> 00:38:21,799
How do they think
this war is going to end?

575
00:38:22,667 --> 00:38:26,237
I found that virtually nobody

576
00:38:26,570 --> 00:38:30,408
would answer that question
in a concrete way.

577
00:38:30,608 --> 00:38:35,946
No one would say
what the final arrangement will look like.

578
00:38:36,314 --> 00:38:39,784
At some point
we're going to get a frozen conflict.

579
00:38:40,318 --> 00:38:43,087
So what's the frozen conflict look like?

580
00:38:43,087 --> 00:38:46,290
What are the territorial lines?

581
00:38:46,624 --> 00:38:50,261
What are the specifics regarding

582
00:38:50,261 --> 00:38:54,365
Ukraine being a neutral state
and so forth and so on.

583
00:38:54,665 --> 00:38:57,368
And nobody could come up

584
00:38:57,368 --> 00:39:00,371
with a concrete answer to that.

585
00:39:00,604 --> 00:39:04,375
When it be clear the Russians do
believe they're going to win this war.

586
00:39:04,842 --> 00:39:09,747
But exactly what winning means 
is not clear.

587
00:39:09,814 --> 00:39:14,985
Now, it's very important
that I emphasize that nobody in Russia

588
00:39:15,453 --> 00:39:20,958
ever said to me that they envisioned
conquering all of Ukraine.

589
00:39:21,092 --> 00:39:23,127
I find this hardly surprising.

590
00:39:23,127 --> 00:39:27,098
The Russians would be foolish
in the extreme to try to conquer all

591
00:39:27,098 --> 00:39:32,203
of Ukraine, because the ethnic Ukrainians
in the western half of that country

592
00:39:32,303 --> 00:39:36,107
would make any occupation
of all of Ukraine a total nightmare.

593
00:39:36,407 --> 00:39:41,846
So the Russians are not conquering 
or bent on conquering all of Ukraine.

594
00:39:42,146 --> 00:39:45,449
But they are going to take a large slice
of territory.

595
00:39:45,483 --> 00:39:46,484
And my sense

596
00:39:46,484 --> 00:39:50,321
talking to people, there were a few people
who said this out loud, but not many.

597
00:39:50,321 --> 00:39:54,658
But my sense talking to people
is that they would prefer to conquer

598
00:39:54,658 --> 00:39:58,062
more territory than just the four oblasts

599
00:39:58,062 --> 00:40:01,732
that they've annexed,
which I was talking about before.

600
00:40:01,766 --> 00:40:05,202
I think that there are a number
of Russians who would like to take Odessa,

601
00:40:05,636 --> 00:40:11,041
and take Kharkiv
and a handful of other oblasts as well.

602
00:40:11,442 --> 00:40:16,580
The Russians would like to push 
as far west as possible,

603
00:40:17,148 --> 00:40:20,151
in large part because they believe

604
00:40:20,518 --> 00:40:24,021
that the remaining rump state

605
00:40:24,054 --> 00:40:27,158
of Ukraine will be hostile to Russia.

606
00:40:27,691 --> 00:40:32,363
So you see, what's happening here
is that the Russians understand

607
00:40:32,863 --> 00:40:36,267
that the Ukrainians
are never going to be happy

608
00:40:37,334 --> 00:40:40,171
with the status quo that is

609
00:40:40,171 --> 00:40:43,908
set in place after the shooting stops.

610
00:40:44,475 --> 00:40:47,545
The Ukrainians are going to want
their territory back.

611
00:40:47,745 --> 00:40:51,849
They're going to want those oblasts
that they lost to Russia back,

612
00:40:52,116 --> 00:40:56,253
and they're going to go to great
lengths to try to undermine

613
00:40:56,287 --> 00:41:00,024
Russian control of the territory

614
00:41:00,024 --> 00:41:03,027
that Russia takes away from Ukraine.

615
00:41:03,093 --> 00:41:06,163
This incentivizes the Russians to take

616
00:41:06,197 --> 00:41:09,200
as much territory as possible.

617
00:41:09,366 --> 00:41:11,902
As much territory is

618
00:41:11,902 --> 00:41:14,839
as is militarily feasible

619
00:41:14,839 --> 00:41:18,342
to conquer
and politically feasible to occupy.

620
00:41:19,043 --> 00:41:23,481
And again, where those lines are drawn
is hard to say at this point in time.

621
00:41:23,481 --> 00:41:28,118
But again, the Russians are incentivized
to move as far west as possible,

622
00:41:28,385 --> 00:41:32,623
and they're incentivized
to make sure that the rump Ukrainian state

623
00:41:32,623 --> 00:41:38,195
that remains is as dysfunctional
as possible.

624
00:41:38,229 --> 00:41:41,532
And again,
a number of Russians would say that

625
00:41:41,532 --> 00:41:44,902
publicly or say that to me out loud.

626
00:41:44,902 --> 00:41:47,705
But it was implicit in the comments of

627
00:41:49,340 --> 00:41:52,676
a number
of other Russians that I talked to.

628
00:41:52,877 --> 00:41:57,414
So I think that

629
00:41:57,414 --> 00:42:00,417
there's no question that

630
00:42:00,651 --> 00:42:05,856
the settlement that the Russians envision
imposing on Ukraine

631
00:42:06,023 --> 00:42:08,859
will be a harsh settlement, for sure.

632
00:42:08,859 --> 00:42:11,629
Now I want to make two final points.

633
00:42:11,629 --> 00:42:14,565
One has to do with denazification.

634
00:42:14,565 --> 00:42:19,703
The Russians talk
a lot about denazification in Ukraine,

635
00:42:19,937 --> 00:42:24,742
and this means eliminating the hard line,

636
00:42:24,808 --> 00:42:27,811
right wing, highly nationalistic,

637
00:42:29,480 --> 00:42:30,814
Ukrainian elites

638
00:42:30,814 --> 00:42:34,685
from positions of power
inside the Ukrainian government.

639
00:42:35,286 --> 00:42:38,289
I personally do not see

640
00:42:38,422 --> 00:42:41,325
how the Russians can do that.

641
00:42:41,325 --> 00:42:45,763
As I've tried to make clear here,
we are going to end up with

642
00:42:45,763 --> 00:42:49,934
a Ukrainian rub state and it's going to be
a dysfunctional rum state.

643
00:42:50,167 --> 00:42:54,305
But I do not believe that the Russians
will be in a position

644
00:42:54,338 --> 00:42:58,142
where they can determine
who runs that Rump state.

645
00:42:58,509 --> 00:43:02,479
And I think once this war is over with,
the nationalists

646
00:43:02,646 --> 00:43:08,252
will be a powerful political force
in Ukraine.

647
00:43:08,319 --> 00:43:11,055
And maybe I should say
the ultra nationalist

648
00:43:11,055 --> 00:43:14,091
will be a powerful political force
in Ukraine.

649
00:43:14,358 --> 00:43:19,263
And those ultra nationalists
will fit the description

650
00:43:19,296 --> 00:43:23,300
of the Russians as Nazis.

651
00:43:23,701 --> 00:43:26,770
But I don't believe
the Russians will be able to.

652
00:43:27,104 --> 00:43:28,806
And I'm using the Russian rhetoric.

653
00:43:28,806 --> 00:43:30,407
Denazification, Ukraine.

654
00:43:30,407 --> 00:43:35,646
I think the the hardline nationalists
will remain in power

655
00:43:35,946 --> 00:43:39,049
in significant ways inside of Ukraine.

656
00:43:40,084 --> 00:43:43,220
And my final point is that

657
00:43:43,821 --> 00:43:47,024
in the West, it's widely believed

658
00:43:47,024 --> 00:43:50,294
that not only are the Russians interested

659
00:43:50,461 --> 00:43:54,531
and conquering all of Ukraine,
but they're a threat to Eastern Europe,

660
00:43:54,865 --> 00:43:58,802
that the Russians
want to basically restore

661
00:43:58,802 --> 00:44:03,574
the Soviet empire in Eastern Europe
if they can, and that they have.

662
00:44:04,308 --> 00:44:07,344
A well-tuned army

663
00:44:07,344 --> 00:44:10,347
at this point in time that they can use
for that purpose.

664
00:44:10,347 --> 00:44:14,118
Now, this is to say that the West,

665
00:44:14,284 --> 00:44:18,956
especially the Europeans, are facing
a significant threat from Russia.

666
00:44:19,423 --> 00:44:21,225
I have never believed that.

667
00:44:21,225 --> 00:44:26,296
And from talking to the Russians
on this visit,

668
00:44:26,563 --> 00:44:29,767
I see no evidence, none,

669
00:44:30,067 --> 00:44:36,006
that the Russians are interested
in conquering any territory in Europe.

670
00:44:36,373 --> 00:44:40,978
As I said before, I saw no evidence,
not even a hint,

671
00:44:41,545 --> 00:44:46,116
that the Russians were interested
in conquering all of Ukraine.

672
00:44:46,617 --> 00:44:49,486
And as I said,
they would be foolish to try to do that,

673
00:44:49,486 --> 00:44:52,489
and they would be foolish to try to

674
00:44:52,823 --> 00:44:56,960
invade territory
in Eastern Europe or countries

675
00:44:56,960 --> 00:45:00,597
in Eastern Europe,
which just happened to be members of NATO,

676
00:45:00,898 --> 00:45:04,668
which causes all sorts of obvious problem.

677
00:45:04,868 --> 00:45:08,539
So I think the only interesting question
on the table now

678
00:45:08,539 --> 00:45:13,043
regarding where this war ends up
is, number one,

679
00:45:13,077 --> 00:45:16,647
how much territory do
the Russians take in Ukraine?

680
00:45:16,847 --> 00:45:19,183
In other words,
where do they stop moving west?

681
00:45:19,183 --> 00:45:23,887
Number two, what is Ukraine's

682
00:45:23,887 --> 00:45:26,890
relationship with the West moving forward?

683
00:45:27,057 --> 00:45:30,094
Will Ukraine declare its neutrality,

684
00:45:30,227 --> 00:45:33,497
or will it continue to posture

685
00:45:33,530 --> 00:45:36,734
about joining NATO?

686
00:45:37,434 --> 00:45:43,107
But regardless
of what happens on that front,

687
00:45:43,340 --> 00:45:46,777
I think the Russians will be incentivized

688
00:45:47,077 --> 00:45:50,347
to do everything they can to turn

689
00:45:50,347 --> 00:45:53,484
Ukraine into a dysfunctional rump state

690
00:45:53,584 --> 00:45:56,820
and keep it as a dysfunctional rump state.

691
00:45:57,588 --> 00:46:02,459
All of this is to say that
this war has been one giant disaster.

692
00:46:02,559 --> 00:46:07,664
And as I have said many times,
the decision in early April of 2008

693
00:46:07,664 --> 00:46:13,604
to expand NATO into Ukraine,
to bring Ukraine into the alliance,

694
00:46:13,737 --> 00:46:18,942
was one of the greatest strategic
blunders of modern times.

695
00:46:19,610 --> 00:46:21,945
That concludes what I have to say

696
00:46:21,945 --> 00:46:24,948
about my recent visit to Russia.

697
00:46:25,149 --> 00:46:30,420
Thank you all for listening
and I look forward to our next episode.

